## What is topical authority?

Topical authority describes a website's depth of useful coverage and demonstrated expertise within a subject. I use it to plan which customer questions a business should answer and how those answers connect to its services. It is an SEO planning concept. It is not a public Google score or a guarantee that every related page will rank.

My topical authority service starts with the content and search demand you already have. I identify missing answers, overlapping pages and original evidence that readers currently struggle to find. The goal is a useful content network that supports relevant enquiries.

## What does a topical authority project include?

A topical authority project produces a prioritised content plan around your business's subject and customer decisions. The scope is agreed around the existing site, audience and resources available to implement it.

- **Demand and coverage review:** compare real search queries and customer questions with the answers already on your site.
- **Page ownership:** decide which existing URL should answer each intent before proposing a new page.
- **Content architecture:** organise useful topics into related sections and identify gaps or consolidation opportunities.
- **Briefs and internal links:** specify the answer, evidence and connections each priority page needs.
- **Measurement:** track relevant queries, landing pages and enquiry events separately from publishing volume.

The [topical map service](/services/topical-maps/) focuses on the architecture deliverable. This service covers the wider process of improving and measuring that coverage.

## How do I measure progress?

I measure topical coverage through the questions your pages answer. Search Console shows whether the intended URLs gain impressions and clicks for relevant queries. Analytics shows what visitors do next. Neither system provides a verified topical authority score.

Historical data, topical coverage and retrieval cost are useful lenses in semantic SEO methodology. Their relationship is not a published Google ranking equation. A formula cannot establish that your site has authority or forecast the traffic a new page will earn.

Google's [people-first content guidance](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content) emphasises useful information and reliable evidence. I use that principle to check the plan rather than treating a page count as the goal.

## How many pages do you need?

A topical authority project has no universal minimum page count. The required coverage depends on the customer questions, the complexity of the subject and what your existing pages already answer.

One complete guide may answer several connected questions. Separate pages are useful when a question has a distinct purpose and enough evidence to justify its own answer. I avoid publishing thin pages for every query variation. Existing pages are improved before new pages are commissioned where they already own the intent.

## How long does topical authority work take?

Planning and implementation times depend on the agreed scope and your publishing capacity. Search results also depend on competition, crawl and index access, the starting site and the quality of the work. I do not promise a ranking milestone at three, six or twelve months.

Early checks establish whether changes are accessible and indexed. Later reviews compare query coverage, clicks and recorded enquiries over complete measurement windows. Release dates and other changes are logged because a before-and-after comparison alone cannot establish causation.

## How do content and technical SEO work together?

Useful coverage needs accessible pages. Crawl restrictions, duplicate URLs and unclear internal links can make the content harder to find. Technical improvements support discovery. They do not establish subject expertise on their own.

The [technical SEO audit](/services/technical-seo-audit/) investigates crawl and index issues. Content work then supplies the answers and evidence that visitors need on the accessible pages.

## How does topical authority differ from Domain Authority?

Topical authority describes subject coverage and expertise. Domain Authority is a third-party link-based metric from Moz. They measure different ideas and neither guarantees Google rankings.

I prioritise content gaps and business-relevant questions while considering technical access and the site's wider reputation. A low or high third-party score does not replace examining the actual pages.

## What is the next step?

A free 20-minute SEO diagnosis helps identify your biggest content or search problem and the next useful investigation. It does not include a full topical map or written audit. Those deliverables are scoped separately.

